# Vulnerability details

## Impact
A malicious user can deposit recursively by using borrowed USDS so he can receive more reward than usual.
And this can decrease value of USDS.


## Proof of Concept
A user can borrow USDS in proportion of deposited collateral.
But a malicious user can swap USDS to collateral and deposit recursively.
This means that he can deposit at most about 150% of collateral than initially owned.
But liquidity reward is calculated only in proportion of collateral regardless of borrowed amount.
So he can receive more reward than usual.

`StakingRewards.sol#userRewardForPool` function which calculates liquidity reward for a user is as follows.
```solidity
	function userRewardForPool( address wallet, bytes32 poolID ) public view returns (uint256)
		{
		// If there are no shares for the pool, the user can't have any shares either and there can't be any rewards
		if ( totalShares[poolID] == 0 )
			return 0;

		UserShareInfo memory user = _userShareInfo[wallet][poolID];
		if ( user.userShare == 0 )
			return 0;

		// Determine the share of the rewards for the user based on their deposited share
243		uint256 rewardsShare = ( totalRewards[poolID] * user.userShare ) / totalShares[poolID];

		// Reduce by the virtualRewards - as they were only added to keep the share / rewards ratio the same when the used added their share

		// In the event that virtualRewards exceeds rewardsShare due to precision loss - just return zero
		if ( user.virtualRewards > rewardsShare )
			return 0;

		return rewardsShare - user.virtualRewards;
		}
```
As we can see above, the amount of reward is related only to the value of collateral.

On the other hand, there is not interest on loan and no swap fee so a malicious user to deposit recursively doesn't have any problem.

This can decrease usds by rate of [wbtc/usds], [weth/usds] pool.

