### Vulnerability Detail

In the DAO contract, the `_executeApproval` function updates the `excludedCountries` mapping based on the type of governance ballot.

The function directly changes the state of the `excludedCountries` mapping without ensuring the atomicity of these updates.

The update is conditional on the ballot type (`INCLUDE_COUNTRY` or `EXCLUDE_COUNTRY`), which determines whether a country is marked as excluded or included.

### Code Snippet

```solidity
    else if (ballot.ballotType == BallotType.INCLUDE_COUNTRY) {
        excludedCountries[ballot.string1] = false;
        emit GeoExclusionUpdated(ballot.string1, false, exchangeConfig.accessManager().geoVersion());
    }
    else if (ballot.ballotType == BallotType.EXCLUDE_COUNTRY) {
        excludedCountries[ballot.string1] = true;
        exchangeConfig.accessManager().excludedCountriesUpdated();
        emit GeoExclusionUpdated(ballot.string1, true, exchangeConfig.accessManager().geoVersion());
    }
```

### Impact

If a transaction fails after partially updating the `excludedCountries` mapping (due to reversion or failure in subsequent operations like `exchangeConfig.accessManager().excludedCountriesUpdated()`), it could lead to an inconsistent state. For instance, a country might be incorrectly marked as excluded or included contrary to the governance decision.

Such inconsistencies can undermine the integrity of the governance process, as the contract state may not accurately reflect the decisions made by DAO participants.

Incorrect entries in the `excludedCountries` mapping can lead to improper access control, potentially allowing or denying users based on inaccurate geographic restrictions.

